rt that meet the different needs  and working styles of different people  might have and when people on your team  get into the habit of valuing each other  as unique individuals that internal  culture can actually translate into  building more equitable less biased  products for your users so we like to  say that policies should come from  overarching principles and one of the  most important is aiming for inclusion  and equity not just diversity we haven't  really defined those terms yet so here's  how I think about it I think if  diversity is about being about headcount  and representation as an example do you  do user research with people with  disabilities equity is about resources  and opportunity do you compensate people  with disabilities at a fair and just  level considering their unique  perspective their investment and giving  you that feedback and the extra value  that they're adding to your product an  inclusion is the hardest to measure it's  about how marginalized people feel about  your culture do people with disabilities  come away feeling like valued partners  do you create an environment where they  can provide honest feedback and you  listen and act on their feedback so  let's give you some examples to improve  the inclusion and equity within your  organization you can work at many levels  so you could write more inclusive job  descriptions a cool - cool tool here is  text EO which I think one of our  previous speakers actually works at  right now which points out masculine and  feminine word ease and helps you shift  the balance in your job description so  that everyone feels comfortable applying  you could also expand benefits beyond  just young single employees one example  is allowing flexible hours for a child  or elder care or you can make make  remote work an option for everyone  you should also account for power  dynamics  during conflict resolution this can  relieve some pressure from marginalized  people who may feel trapped or powerles